  "throughputHeading": "Sustained throughput and the latency tail, 128 to 2048 concurrent",
  "throughputLede": "Closed-loop: N requests held in flight continuously against one engine, 30s measured per point (after a 10s warm-up). Left charts are sustained requests/sec; right charts are p99 latency. One line per engine, faceted by payload size — the three sizes behave very differently, which is the whole point.",
  "tableNote": "Each row is one measured 30s steady-state cell. Same policy, same corpus, same driver to both engines. req/s is sustained throughput; p-columns are the latency distribution; errors are failed requests (timeouts/resets) under load.",
  "takeaways": [
    {
      "title": "Small, frequent payloads are the FPGA's home turf",
      "body": "At 2.6 KB, Themis sustains ~29k req/s versus RE2's ~17k — up to 1.7× — and its p99 stays 2.5× tighter (34 ms vs 87 ms at 512 concurrency). Themis is higher-throughput AND lower-latency at every concurrency level: the fixed hardware pipeline absorbs request rate where the CPU engine's tail climbs."
    },
    {
      "title": "The edge narrows to parity as payloads grow",
      "body": "At 34 KB both engines plateau near 5–6k req/s, Themis ~15% ahead. The per-request byte cost — transport, not matching — starts to dominate and both engines pay it, so medium-payload latency is roughly a wash."
    },
    {
      "title": "Large payloads are bandwidth-bound — mind the concurrency ceiling",
      "body": "At 293 KB both engines saturate by ~128 concurrency (~135–159 MiB/s); adding connections past that only inflates latency. Themis holds a modestly higher ceiling through 1024, then collapses at 2048 (17k errors) where RE2 degrades gracefully. Provision large-payload concurrency below that cliff — it is the byte-bound front end, not the matcher."
    }
  ],
  "meaning": {
    "heading": "Where the FPGA wins, and where it doesn't"
  },
  "bounds": [
    {
      "title": "Same policy, corpus, driver",
      "body": "every engine gets the identical 5,000-rule listMatch policy and the same request bodies from one driver; a throughput test on the same literal policy, not a new capability claim."
    },
    {
      "title": "End-to-end, not engine-isolated",
      "body": "each latency includes network + TLS + the engine's HTTP front end; no server-side timing hook is exposed. Connections are pooled (HTTP/1.1 keep-alive) so TLS is amortized, but on large payloads the front end — not the matching core — is the limiter (see the concurrency-1 probe: matching is effectively free)."
    },
    {
      "title": "Large @2048 is a Themis front-end limit, not the matcher",
      "body": "Themis's large-payload cell collapsed at 2048 concurrency (17,225 errors, ~2 req/s) while RE2 limped through (2,185 errors). Consistent with the probe: Themis's large-payload cost is byte transport (~1.4 MB/s per connection), which has a hard parallel-transfer ceiling. Below 2048 it held a higher throughput ceiling than RE2."
    },
    {
